`\"ima4\"`. Only reached after a `data` chunk broke the loop. | `ret -3` |\n\n## Absent checks (the C does *not* reject; behaviour still observable)\n\n| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result |\n|---|----------|----------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n| 4 | `ima_parse` | `data == NULL` (no null check on `data`) | `SIGSEGV` reading `*(u32*)0` |\n| 5 | `ima_parse` | `info == NULL`, input otherwise fully valid (no null check on `info`) | `SIGSEGV` writing `*(void**)0` |\n| 6 | `ima_parse` | `data == NULL` **and** `info == NULL` | `SIGSEGV` (read of `data` faults first) |\n| 7 | `ima_parse` | a `data` chunk is reached with **no preceding `desc` chunk** → `desc` is still `NULL` and `desc->format_id` is dereferenced | `SIGSEGV` reading `*(u32*)0x8` |\n| 8 | `ima_parse` | a `data` chunk is reached with a valid `ima4` `desc` but **no preceding `pakt` chunk** → `pakt` is `NULL` and `pakt->frame_count` is dereferenced. Note `info->blocks` and `info->size` are stored *before* the fault. | `SIGSEGV` reading `*(u64*)0x8` |\n| 9 | `ima_parse` | **no `pakt` chunk and `desc->format_id != 'ima4'`** — the `-3` check happens *before* `pakt` is touched, so this returns cleanly instead of faulting. Ordering-sensitive; a Rust version that validated `pakt` early would diverge. | `ret -3` (no fault) |\n| 10 | `ima_parse` | valid header, but the buffer contains **no `data` chunk** (only unknown chunk types with `size >= 0`) → `for(;;)` never breaks and walks past the end of the buffer | `hang` then `SIGSEGV` (unmapped page) |\n| 11 | `ima_parse` | `chunk_size == -16` on a non-`desc`/`pakt`/`data` chunk → `chunk += 16 + (-16)` leaves `chunk` unchanged → tight infinite loop, no forward progress check | `hang` (forever) |\n| 12 | `ima_parse` | `chunk_size < 0` (e.g. `-32`) → the chunk pointer walks *backwards*; no lower-bound check | no rejection; walks backwards, eventually `SIGSEGV` |\n| 13 | `ima_parse` | `chunk_size == INT64_MAX` / `INT64_MIN` → pointer arithmetic overflows; no range check | no rejection; wild pointer, `SIGSEGV` |\n| 14 | `ima_parse` | `chunk_size` one step past every \"reasonable\" bound (`0`, `1`, `2^32`, `2^47`) on a `data` chunk. `info->size` is set from this raw value with **no validation**, and is the `s64`->`u64` bit-preserving reinterpretation of it. | `ret 0`, `info->size == (u64)chunk_size` |\n| 15 | `ima_parse` | `header->flags` set to any of `0..=0xffff` — the field is **never read**; no rejection may depend on it | `ret 0` (flags ignored) |\n| 16 | `ima_parse` | `chunk->type` = any of the 2^32 values that is **not** `'desc'`/`'pakt'`/`'data'`, including one-bit-off near-misses of each. This is the API's only \"open discriminant\"; there is no `enum` in this ABI, so this is the closest analogue of an out-of-range enum value crossing FFI. All fall through to the advance path with no rejection. | no rejection; `chunk += 16 + size` |\n| 17 | `ima_parse` | `desc->sample_rate` bytes forming a `double` that is negative, `NaN`, `±Inf`, `>= 2^63`, or `>= 2^64` when read as a native (little-endian) `double`. The C then does a `double`->`unsigned long long` *value conversion*, which C leaves **undefined** for all of these. No check exists. | no rejection; `ret 0` with the x86-64 `cvttsd2si` result (`0x8000000000000000` \"integer indefinite\", then byte-swapped and bit-cast) |\n| 18 | `ima_parse` | `desc->channels_per_frame == 0`, `1`, `0xffffffff` — no range check on channel count | `ret 0`, `info->channel_count == bswap32(raw)` |\n| 19 | `ima_parse` | `pakt->frame_count` negative / `INT64_MIN` / `INT64_MAX` — no range check | `ret 0`, `info->frame_count == bswap64(raw)` |\n| 20 | `ima_parse` | Truncated buffer: valid `caff` + version but fewer than 16 bytes of chunk present, so the first `chunk->type`/`chunk->size` load reads past the allocation | no rejection; reads OOB (no length parameter exists to check) |\n\n## Ordering constraints that must be preserved\n\n