ring the return code and all **40 raw bytes** of `ima_info` (including tail padding, and `blocks` as an absolute pointer, since both get the same buffer).\n- **Phase C** — 20/20 rows pass, including 8 fault/hang rows run in re-exec'd child processes.\n- **Phase D** — `nm -D` diff is empty (`ima_parse` only) in every configuration.\n\n## Two real bugs found and fixed\n\n**1. Wrong signal on null/wild pointers** (rows 4–8 initially failed). The translation used `ptr::read_unaligned`, which is wrong for reproducing a C raw load in two independent ways: it forwards to `copy_nonoverlapping`, whose `debug_assertions`-only \"is not null\" precondition fires `panic_nounwind` → **SIGABRT** where C gives **SIGSEGV**; and with optimization on, a plain load from a provably-invalid pointer is UB that LLVM **deletes outright** — I measured the release build exiting 0 with no fault at all. Same problem on the `(*info).field = …` stores. Both now use `read_volatile`/`write_volatile` through an `align == 1` `#[repr(C, packed)]` wrapper: alignment is trivially satisfied (so misaligned bases still work), there's no null precondition, and volatile accesses can't be elided.\n\nThis is exactly the class of bug the dev/release split exposes — neither profile alone would have caught it.\n\n**2. My own test bugs, not the Rust's.** 7 Phase B failures were in my expected-value assertions (I double-applied the byte swap — the builder writes big-endian and the C's `btoh` undoes it). And my first `row20` segfaulted the test process because the walk only ever visits offsets ≡ 8 mod 16, so my planted stop chunk was missed. Worth flagging: `assert_same` never fired in either case, so C and Rust already agreed.\n\n## Notable verification depth\n\nThe riskiest line is `conv64.u = desc->sample_rate` — a **float→integer value conversion**, not a bit cast, which is UB for negative/NaN/Inf/≥2^64 inputs. The Rust emulates GCC's `comisd`/`jae`/`cvttsd2si`/`subsd`/`xor` lowering. I fuzzed it with 10⁶ inputs